{
  "_note": "Known dangerous forms, split by what the rules do with each one today. All three lists are asserted, in both directions, so a form moving between them shows up in the tests instead of silently. `denied` is the half that must never shrink: the catastrophic forms, here and on another host. `asked` is what still reaches the person — ruling P45 keeps destructive forms (including over ssh/remote-exec) and the guard's own decision surface. `allowed` is what passes on purpose, for two separate reasons: P43 stopped treating unreadability as a verdict (a destructive command behind a pipe, a dynamic head word or an interpreter payload is not caught), and P45 retired the asks that were not destructive forms (git push, local history rewrites, key reads, global installs, sudo, chmod 777, curl with a body, a public IP, a git exec option).",
